     "893e10de-6cbf-46cf-a415-4c3a4f6894ef"], Cell["\<\
quotients of the hyperbolic tiling, infinitely many of each type\
\>", "TableText",ExpressionUUID->"e2e643eb-042e-4b03-ad34-f9f9f7a0397e"]}
  }]], "3ColumnTableMod",ExpressionUUID->"50cd0429-2eaf-45bb-a267-\
989756ef9243"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The regular maps of type {",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 "} are the quotients ",
 StyleBox["G", "TI"],
 " of the triangle group by its normal subgroups of finite index. The \
triangle group is generated by ",
 StyleBox["x", "TI"],
 " of order ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 ", which turns a face, and ",
 StyleBox["y", "TI"],
 " of order ",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 ", which turns a vertex, with ",
 StyleBox["x", "TI"],
 " ",
 StyleBox["y", "TI"],
 " of order 2, which reverses an edge. The map of ",
 StyleBox["G", "TI"],
 " has |",
 StyleBox["G", "TI"],
 "|/",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 " vertices, |",
 StyleBox["G", "TI"],
 "|/2 edges and |",
 StyleBox["G", "TI"],
 "|/",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " faces. So the maps of a type in increasing order of size are its normal \
subgroups in increasing order of index, and the ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-th map is the ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-th of them. They are found by an enumeration of the normal subgroups up to \
the order 1100; a type with fewer than ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " maps up to that order leaves the call unevaluated."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c62e70d5-8fca-4384-a424-3ac9e0a56200"],

Cell["\<\
Two conventions fix the count. Each normal subgroup is one map, so a chiral \
pair counts twice, and a map and its dual are distinct unless the map is \
self-dual. A quotient whose skeleton has a loop or a multiple edge is \
skipped, so every map is a simple graph: the {3, 8} map of order 48, on the \
Bolza surface, has 6 vertices and 24 edges and is left out.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"455b6daa-5308-47bb-a870-1368f65d2f28"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The Euler characteristic ",
 StyleBox["V", "TI"],
 " \[Minus] ",
 StyleBox["E", "TI"],
 " + ",
 StyleBox["F", "TI"],
 " with ",
 StyleBox["F", "TI"],
 " = 2",
 StyleBox["E", "TI"],
 "/",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " reads the surface off the graph: 2 on the sphere, 0 on the torus, negative \
on a hyperbolic surface."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"8e12d021-5736-4132-9d50-7d2915dbbfca"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Every map here is ",
 StyleBox["closed",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". A line or a circle drawn on it wraps round the surface, and on a small \
map the wrap is felt within a few steps. For plane geometry use the patches \
of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraSubstrate"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSubstrate", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "785c357e-348d-47c4-b122-08056f5ba4ce"],
 ", which are cut from the infinite tiling and have no wrap."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a9ac4713-9179-479e-b62e-21df10aaf15c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The flat tori are ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["TorusTessellation"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/TorusTessellation", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "f448c939-4466-4338-8f7b-965a0a34a3ea"],
 " graphs. A uniform map is built by the Conway operations rectify and \
truncate, and their composites, on the ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 "-th map of its parent type, using the faces of that map: the orbits of the \
face rotation on a quotient, the lattice cells on a torus. The eleven \
Archimedean solids that these operations reach are computed from the Platonic \
solids; the prisms and antiprisms are built directly. The two snub solids, \
the snub and elongated tilings, and a torus too small to carry a simple map \
stay unevaluated."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f2835d18-fdc6-4e25-b8e4-a31bba87ff52"],

